{"context":"The EKA line (standard + core + CLI) coordinates work through execution containers: work items (sto-/ts-/bug-/td-/ch-/spk-) join a container (ctr-) through tickets (tkt-), advance along the D1 execution-state table, and pass the R13 note-resolution gates at in-review/done (ADR-019). What the model does not express is **human ownership**: which member of the team is responsible for a given work item. The `by` change-log authority records who changed state, not who owns the work. Team collaboration requires an answer to \"who does this work?\" that is queryable, projectable, and reviewable.\n\nThe planning discussion on 2026-08-15 (evidence `cmt:team-collaboration-implementation-2`) locked the team-collaboration data model in `req:team-collaboration` §3. This ADR records those locked decisions as architecture in force for the EKA line (the requirement is the authority; this ADR is its architecture stratum).\n\nFour gaps drive the decisions:\n\n1. **No member concept.** There is no artifact type for a team member. Work items cannot express responsibility, and nothing can scope work by person.\n2. **No assignment relationship.** The canonical relationship set (supersedes, amends, derives-from, depends-on, validates, discusses, replies-to) cannot express \"this work item is assigned to this member\".\n3. **Review provenance is implicit.** The R13 gates (rule-014.md) read note-state only — they never check *who* authored the satisfying note. An assigned item can be reviewed by anyone, which weakens review semantics for team-owned work.\n4. **Projections have no member scope.** `eka view execution`/board derive container membership from tickets (relationship-only, ADR-013), but there is no member axis — and unassigned work has no explicit bucket in a member-scoped view.","decision":"### Scope of this ADR\n\nThis ADR records the data model locked in `req:team-collaboration` §3: the `mbr-` token family, the `assigned-to` relationship (with single-assignee cardinality, conditional rule mechanics, and same-repo enforcement), member-scoped projection semantics, and the explicit non-goals. Implementation mechanics (validator clauses, view filters, the provenance equality mapping) are execution items under this ADR, not part of the decision.\n\n### Decision 1 — `mbr-` token family (membership artifacts)\n\nA new artifact type token `mbr-` (member) is added to the canonical token table:\n\n- **Home domain**: Execution (stratum 4) — operating-token semantics (informational dimension, `IsKnowledge` false), like `ctr-`/`tkt-`/`ses-`/`cmt-`.\n- **State vector**: content-state + existence-state — the member record carries its own content (member identity/role material) and lifecycle; it owns no work-item state.\n- **R10 exemption**: `mbr-` joins the `tkt-`/`ses-`/`cmt-` exemption in `rule-011.md` (Exceptions) and the matching exemption clause in `conformance/rules_domain.go` (rule10). Rationale is identical: membership artifacts are operating records whose only meaningful edge is the assignment relationship; stratification traceability is not meaningful for them, and the R10 verdict is a warning-level quality signal anyway.\n\n### Decision 2 — `assigned-to` relationship (work item → member)\n\nA new canonical relationship type `assigned-to` expresses assignment:\n\n- **Vocabulary**: `assigned-to` joins the canonical relationship set (specification/relationships.md) and the relationship vocabulary; the conformance engine recognizes it (relationshipFields, conformance/state.go).\n- **Direction and cardinality**: one-directional from the work item to the member; **at-most-one target** — a work item has at most one assignee (single-assignee rule). A member may be the target of many inbound `assigned-to` edges.\n- **R5 entry**: `assigned-to` references resolve like every canonical relationship — with **R5-mirrored draft tolerance**: an unresolved `assigned-to` on a draft artifact is a warning, never a violation (a draft work item may point at a draft member).\n- **Conditional R13 sub-check with provenance equality**: when a work item carries an `assigned-to` edge, the note(s) satisfying the R13 gate must have the **same provenance as the assignee** — the gate-satisfying note's author must equal the assigned member's identity. The sub-check is conditional: work items without an assignee keep the plain R13 gate unchanged.\n- **Same-repo enforcement**: `assigned-to` targets must resolve within the same repository (project namespace) — cross-repository assignment is refused, mirroring the repository-context ownership gate the authoring commands already apply (relate/note/transition).\n\n### Decision 3 — Advisory member-scoped filter projections\n\nThe projection layer (view/board/execution) MAY offer member-scoped filters:\n\n- Derivation is relationship-only: an item's membership in a member's scope is derived from `assigned-to` edges, never from content (ADR-013).\n- **'No assignee' bucket semantics**: work items without any `assigned-to` edge surface in a dedicated \"No assignee\" bucket — never silently excluded from member-scoped views. This mirrors the existing unassigned bucket of the container board projection.\n- The projections are advisory: no conformance rule requires or enforces member scoping; it is a projection feature, not an invariant.\n\n### Decision 4 — Non-Goals (explicit)\n\n- **No authentication/authorization**: `mbr-` is a knowledge record of who works, not a security principal. The engine does not authenticate or authorize members; **ADR-019 D2 (no authN/authZ in the engine) is unchanged**.\n- **No `by` ↔ `mbr-` conformance coupling**: the `by` change-log authority of artifacts is never coerced to, and never validated against, `mbr-` membership. No conformance rule ties artifact authors to member records.","consequences":"- Work items gain explicit human ownership with a minimal surface: one new token and one new relationship type. Existing artifacts' state vectors, transitions, and gates are unchanged.\n- Review evidence becomes provenance-aware only where assignment exists; unassigned items behave exactly as today (backward compatible).\n- Member-scoped views enable per-person coordination (member filters, 'No assignee' triage) without touching artifact content.\n- The R10 exemption keeps `mbr-` artifacts unblocked; the R5-mirrored draft tolerance keeps authoring ergonomic (assignment can be wired before the member unit is published).\n- Cost: the token and relationship touch the standard corpus (tokens table, relationships.md, rule-011.md exceptions, rule-006.md/R5 entry) and the conformance taxonomy (typeTokens, relationshipFields, rules_domain.go exemption clause), plus exchange importer relationship acceptance. All additive — existing units and snapshots remain valid.\n- Risk: advisory projections can drift across implementations if the derivation rule (assigned-to only) is not followed — ADR-013 discipline applies.\n- Risk: provenance equality couples gate outcomes to author identity; the equality mapping between a cmt- note's author and an mbr- identity must be defined explicitly and kept out of authN/authZ semantics (Decision 4).","alternativesConsidered":"1. **Reuse an existing token for members** (ses-, cmt-, rvw-): rejected — wrong semantics; session/note/review records would be overloaded with identity meaning, and their state vectors and gate machinery would misfire (cmt- carries note-state and discusses; ses- is a pure operating log).\n2. **Assignment as a content field on work items** (e.g. `assignee:` in the item content): rejected — ADR-013: projections derive from relationships, never content. A content field cannot be resolved, gated, or projected deterministically, and it is invisible to R5.\n3. **`assigned-to` without an `mbr-` token** (pointing at a bare identifier or email string): rejected — relationships express Identity; a bare string is not an artifact, breaks R5 resolution, and cannot carry state or traceability.\n4. **Strict enforcement** (assignment required, member existence blocking, author↔member conformance): rejected — assignment is collaboration metadata, not a correctness invariant. Conditional gates + advisory projections preserve the \"structural quality signal, never a blocker\" philosophy (R10 verdict) and keep the engine free of authN/authZ (ADR-019 D2)."}